#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151912 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152294 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "58f2bf06-7449-5a66-9662-532ce70a8ff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21879" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation below, for which the corresponding complementary solution is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2,$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants.\n\n$$\nx^2y'' - 7xy' + 12y = -3x^3\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $y_p(x)=x$ or $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation, and then write the general solution." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(0) - 7x(1) + 12(x) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x$ isn't a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x^3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(6x) - 7x(3x^2) + 12(x^3) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{6}+B{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151982 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/id #uuid "bacb284c-88db-5ad5-bd7d-cbe6fbc604df" :question/math-academy-id "e-6789" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{5x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 10y' + 25y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2}=u(x) e^{5x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}={e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u)-10{e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u)+25{e}^{5x}u & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u-10{u}^{′}-50u+25u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{5x}\\neq 0$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu(x) = c_1x+c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = ue^{5x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\left(c_1 x + c_2\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\cdot x = xe^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = e^{10x} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{5x}+Bxe^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "bbf72227-f0da-5dab-88cd-bfed2ed773f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6792" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y'' - 3y'+ 2y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = e^{x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- $y_2(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement.\n\n- To determine whether $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y_1$ and its derivatives into the equation. Computing the first and second derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\ne^{x}  - 3e^{x} + 2e^{x} = 0. \\color{green}\\quad\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_1$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of $y_2$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n4e^{2x}  - 3(2e^{2x})  + 2(e^{2x} ) = 0. \\quad\\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_2$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- Both $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=e^{2x}$ are solutions of the equation. So, the general solution is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$ if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. To check if they are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{x})}^{′}{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian does not equal $0,$ the functions are linearly independent. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct statements are I, II, and III."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151985 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "f41bad20-5a72-5d6d-82c4-cf36f7ac74cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6794" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x^2 y'' + 3xy' + y = 0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{u(x)}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y_{2} = \\dfrac{u(x)}{x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =\\frac{u}{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & =\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}})+3x(\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u+3{u}^{′}x-3u+u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}+{u}^{′}x}{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}x+{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation:\n\n$$\nw' x + w = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = \\dfrac{c_1}{x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = c_1\\ln{x} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = \\dfrac{u}{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{c_1\\ln{x} + c_2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{A}{x} + \\dfrac{B\\ln{x}}{x} = \\dfrac{A + B\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f5c2990-0c8a-584d-bfde-31963f7ff692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+Bt\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263135} {:answer/id #uuid "784e1679-3718-5a06-a425-4c1bcafd5944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+B{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264044} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9700b6-666a-5f29-a8bb-ff7cd542805c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264349} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e28c2f-fe2a-57e6-bebd-5dd0362f05e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+\\frac{B}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265126} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcf23b6-c4a2-5b74-ab79-b90a02e66db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+B{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d9700b6-666a-5f29-a8bb-ff7cd542805c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264349} :answer-field/id #uuid "b667bc8d-3635-50e2-acc5-7b154ab5806c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa1dae64-83b4-59f1-8ce4-3c9cac8a6ed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130028" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-2y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t)t.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}t+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′})+2t({u}^{′}t+u)-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}t+4w=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=ut$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2})t.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot t=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152294 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb229fa-6f9c-5a69-b0d6-2b934a5e8256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312323} {:answer/id #uuid "b478c96f-c991-58c0-95b2-b2821faf285c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A$ and $B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313381} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ca5e06-743e-56c6-9376-f34c234b3e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b478c96f-c991-58c0-95b2-b2821faf285c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A$ and $B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313381} :answer-field/id #uuid "300f091d-60d2-51e6-9901-b231130a3cf9" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314139} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2219bbfa-a73f-5279-82c4-8e4bb0824640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312234} {:answer/id #uuid "4a828f62-93c2-59b4-8af2-329658a5a0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312580} {:answer/id #uuid "9333a372-c254-5d36-8386-b1fcf0f69a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a828f62-93c2-59b4-8af2-329658a5a0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312580} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce1e876-8ad6-5f03-9bb6-591931b8394d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314340} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aa8a5a5-d664-53a8-8a1e-ddeb8fe6c96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312301} {:answer/id #uuid "49de3ce7-2ddc-518c-854e-eafd8a047df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312572} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7b123a-cc84-54a8-9151-605919bba372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312619} {:answer/id #uuid "745b5e39-98d3-52dd-955c-2733b31747be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49de3ce7-2ddc-518c-854e-eafd8a047df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312572} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5001142-fa63-5975-98fc-6aefc3a3fff8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2e9fe0-9d14-5584-9191-0594a5f5e62d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312337} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9fe0aa-cdb6-5e9b-95ba-83e767629260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312621} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0772f-61d7-596b-a138-456da723e79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0772f-61d7-596b-a138-456da723e79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313947} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb3422fc-9101-5049-b9da-89d0ec0f636d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314656} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a7b4ca9-d4f5-5b81-8dfc-3c846dff159a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312298} {:answer/id #uuid "352fdb5e-5751-5d23-aed4-b0c4585bee5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312402} {:answer/id #uuid "5867f2da-f584-5b0e-ac8e-bbe3cffb4923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312683} {:answer/id #uuid "9a599ed7-aafe-501d-b364-42c43da7d22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "352fdb5e-5751-5d23-aed4-b0c4585bee5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312402} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef2b9423-79f0-5dc9-ab2c-4fbd6f9e2d00" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81480660-8b23-5d75-8411-ad962650246b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339876" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=3{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{-x})^{′}=-{e}^{-x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(-{e}^{-x})^{′}={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n({e}^{-x})+5(-{e}^{-x})+4({e}^{-x})=3{e}^{-x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{-x})^{′}={e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})^{′}=-2{e}^{-x}+x{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(-2{e}^{-x}+x{e}^{-x})+5({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})+4(x{e}^{-x})=3{e}^{-x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}+x{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152294 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a1c3417-4481-5683-b5c7-8a111ebef07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149940} {:answer/id #uuid "c928d1f0-ab4a-5419-b56c-5318deed410e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$and$C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149941} {:answer/id #uuid "2f34cf45-5e73-5e21-bb22-be06bc07a6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149942} {:answer/id #uuid "3594a392-68f2-5a5a-ad28-aa038b74bd73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149943}] :answer-field/id #uuid "43104a38-5959-58d8-9496-90b83dbb174c" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149939} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e1094b3-fc43-5250-9d6a-7b7ef61f3d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149945} {:answer/id #uuid "c18bc512-f9f7-5d52-9207-8a9c53bca94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149946}] :answer-field/id #uuid "491f03f7-8c83-5060-9299-001dc9dddab1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149944} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ee31780-968b-51a2-a54b-e98a17d01f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149948} {:answer/id #uuid "5b08268d-a978-5928-9dec-00a65731505e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149949}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0e9372-806f-50fc-8664-fcc9832a1e55" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149947} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4aee4f5-30ff-51e3-95c9-0a6049ac2515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xe^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149951} {:answer/id #uuid "04161055-2da0-57b1-9f2e-92abdd46d580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cxe^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149952} {:answer/id #uuid "349fa7d6-8c11-578f-b118-75b6e2cc5b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149953} {:answer/id #uuid "6037e525-b5ed-523c-99c2-a2f2f12ce516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ce^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149954}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0f848a61-e820-5e6a-8ef1-bc454ad3d323" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149950} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de905be5-2bb7-5a2a-8702-30eb2f3af947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-5x} + Be^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149956} {:answer/id #uuid "01228d01-173d-5352-9de8-174426966545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-5x} + Be^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149957} {:answer/id #uuid "096ab759-bce8-5d8a-b5ce-2a6dc0e491ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{5x} + Be^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149958} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb16b96-ae1d-5556-b059-46452b0db96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{5x} + Be^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149959}] :answer-field/id #uuid "41a0da28-1640-525e-93bc-52f7cb608d46" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149955}] :question/id #uuid "181a7898-7274-5974-9634-f1677406f23f" :question/math-academy-id "q-339880" :question/problem "$y^{″} - 2y^{′} - 15y =-7e^{4x}$\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{5x} + Be^{-3x}$, where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.\nThe function $y_{p}(x) = e^{4x}$\n{{field-1}}\na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nThe function $y_{p}(x) = xe^{4x}$\n{{field-2}}\na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\nwhere\n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320f4b21-2707-5091-a2f6-3f46b3db3db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355103} {:answer/id #uuid "732019b1-4135-57f2-b0d0-0be5c43e75f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355678} {:answer/id #uuid "7905340a-4ac8-558d-bb3d-307b21001f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355722} {:answer/id #uuid "a886961d-560b-5f03-a7b4-90a1a7508fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356157} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d7e1eb-54ff-5d68-969e-6e99529e477b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Bx\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a886961d-560b-5f03-a7b4-90a1a7508fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356157} :answer-field/id #uuid "296c90ca-5225-52de-bd8b-f92f0038d08f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c4c99c3-e164-55a4-8452-aa0c671a6241" :question/math-academy-id "q-90911" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=6w\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ becomes \n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=3\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"}]}
